00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.280
In this video, you see how to implement regularized logistic regression.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:11.660
Just as the gradient update for logistic regression has seemed surprisingly similar to the gradient

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.320
update for linear regression, you find that the gradient descent update for regularized

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.240
logistic regression will also look similar to the update for regularized linear regression.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.680
Let's take a look.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.200
Here's the idea.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.800
We saw earlier that logistic regression can be prone to overfitting if you fit it with

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.840
very high order polynomial features like this.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:39.880
Here z is a high order polynomial that gets passed into the sigmoid function like so to

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:40.880
compute f.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:47.600
And in particular, you can enter with a decision boundary that is overly complex and overfits

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.360
this training set.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.720
More generally, when you train logistic regression with a lot of features, whether polynomial

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:02.180
features or some other features, there can be a higher risk of overfitting.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.800
This was the cost function for logistic regression.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:12.680
If you want to modify it to use regularization, all you need to do is add to it the following

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:13.680
term.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:21.120
Let's add lambda, the regularization parameter, over 2m times the sum from j equals 1 through

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.620
n where n is the number of features as usual of wj squared.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:32.080
So when you minimize this cost function as a function of w and b, it has the effects of

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:39.640
penalizing parameters w1, w2 through wn and preventing them from being too large.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:44.480
And if you do this, then even though you're fitting a high order polynomial with a lot

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.760
of parameters, you still get a decision boundary that looks like this.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.040
Something that looks more reasonable for separating positive and negative examples while also

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.360
generalizing hopefully to new examples, not in the training set.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.160
So when using regularization, even when you have a lot of features, how can you actually

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.280
implement this?

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.460
How can you actually minimize this cost function j of wb that includes the regularization

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.460
term?

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:17.720
Well, let's use gradient descent as before.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.640
So here's the cost function that you want to minimize.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.340
And to implement gradient descent, as before, we'll carry out the following simultaneous

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:31.640
updates over wj and b.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.080
These are the usual update rules for gradient descent.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:40.480
And just like regularized linear regression, when you compute what are these derivative

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.840
terms, the only thing that changes now is that the derivative with respect to wj

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:54.880
is this additional term, lambda over m times wj added here at the end.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.840
And again, it looks a lot like the update for regularized linear regression.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.440
In fact, it's the exact same equation, except for the fact that the definition of f is now

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:12.260
no longer the linear function, it is the logistic function applied to z.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:18.180
Similar to linear regression, we will regularize only the parameters wj but not the parameter

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:24.380
b, which is why there's no change to the update you would make for b.

